2026-08-30T10:23Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-08-30T10:25Z claude note filed from the 0264 wrap-up: the arm's two strangest observations are jointly suspicious and neither has a cause. Blocks nothing mechanically, but 0267 must weigh it and two ratifications wait on it in practice.
2026-08-30T11:25Z claude note MECHANISM FOUND for both observations. bench/sweep.py RealExecutor._measure_fidelity and _measure_recall never appended --device to the quant_fidelity.mjs subprocess command (unlike _measure_cost, which always did) -- confirmed directly from the original 0264 campaign's own surviving artifacts: every persisted per-rung vector metadata file (/home/haduong/data/0264-vectors/*.json on padme) records "device": "(runtime default)". transformers.js's Node default (devices.js: DEFAULT_DEVICE = cpu) is CPU. So every fidelity/recall cell in the GPU-arm campaign -- labelled auto or cuda purely as sweep-plan bookkeeping -- silently ran on CPU regardless of the requested device. This explains observation 1 (the 58-256 ms/passage figures were genuine CPU rates, not GPU rates) and observation 2 (the CPU arm 0263 and the "GPU arm" 0264 fidelity vectors were byte-identical because both literally ran the same CPU code path, not because of cross-provider fp invariance). POSITIVE CONTROL: nvidia-smi dmon during a real llama-server completion call showed sm% rising 0 to 31-57% and returning to 0, proving the instrumentation discriminates busy from idle (bench/results/0481-gpu-anomaly/dmon/dmon_control_busy.txt). GPU UTILIZATION DURING EMBED (device forwarded correctly, via a new standalone probe verification/probes/gpu_anomaly_embed_probe.mjs): granite-97m/device:cuda/q8 and multilingual-e5-small/device:auto/q8 both showed genuine GPU business (sm% 20-29% and 3-25% respectively, framebuffer memory allocated) throughout the embed call -- GPU is not idle once the flag reaches the driver. ORT PROFILING (enableProfiling, one run each): for q8, MatMulInteger -- the quantized matmul, 69% of node time -- runs entirely on CPUExecutionProvider (CUDA registers no int8 matmul kernel, confirming DEVICE-AUTO-0264.md Result 1's own finding); for fp32, 99.9% of node time is on CUDAExecutionProvider and total kernel time is small (145 ms for 80 rows) -- genuinely GPU-accelerated. BATCH-SIZE SWEEP (1/8/32/128, multilingual-e5-small, device cuda explicit): flat for both q8 (52-66 ms/passage) and fp32 (22-25 ms/passage) -- batch-1 per-call overhead, the ticket's own "boring candidate", is NOT the mechanism; fp32 is consistently ~2.3x faster than q8 on this host because of the MatMulInteger-on-CPU bottleneck. BYTE-IDENTITY DISCRIMINATION: same 20-row stride sample (corpus sha256 949d5af1...eec0ca4, verified identical on both machines), embedded locally on doudou (CPU, no GPU present) and on padme with --device cuda explicit plus profiling: the padme run genuinely executed on CUDA (106,13 of 106,19 ms node time, 99,9%, on CUDAExecutionProvider) and its vector is NOT byte-identical to the CPU vector -- differs from the fourth significant digit (e.g. 0,24527722597122192 CPU vs 0,245431050658226 GPU), cosine 0,9999999283 over the compared dims, max abs diff 1,78e-4 -- exactly the expected signature of genuine cross-provider floating point, and the direct contrast case for 0264's X8 exact-identity verdict. R30 THROUGHPUT: multilingual-e5-small at fp32, device cuda explicit, batch 8, 600 rows (matching 0264's fidelity-cell conditions) measured **21,8 ms/passage**, versus the buggy 59,3 ms/passage 0264 recorded for the identical (model, dtype, rows) under the CPU-only path; projects to **33,8 minutes** for the 93 022-passage corpus -- inside gpu-feasibility.json's superseded 7-95 minute PyTorch-derived band. Flat across batch sizes (above), so this figure is not batch-size-sensitive within 1-128. CAVEAT: measured for ONE candidate at ONE dtype with a correctly-forwarded device, not a full re-run of the six-candidate x four-dtype campaign; q8 measured SLOWER than fp32 on this stack (MatMulInteger-on-CPU), so the deployed-dtype figure needs its own GPU-corrected measurement before ticket 0267 pins anything -- recommend re-running the fidelity/recall sweep with this branch's bench/sweep.py fix before trusting any bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/ ms_per_passage figure as a GPU number. HARNESS FIX in this branch: bench/sweep.py now forwards --device in _measure_fidelity (both fp32 reference and dtype rung) and _measure_recall; regression test tests/test_sweep_real_executor_device.py, verified red against the unfixed code (positive control) before being verified green against the fix. VRAM STARVATION RULED UNLIKELY FOR 0264 BY TIMELINE: llama-server was killed before any 0264 cell ran, GPU confirmed quiet 89/2 MiB, and it was restarted only after the last cell -- so contention for VRAM is not an alternative explanation for either observation; the mechanism above (a missing CLI flag, unrelated to memory pressure) is the full explanation. Full evidence: bench/results/0481-gpu-anomaly/ (SUMMARY.json ties every step together), verification/GPU-ANOMALY-0481.md, verification/probes/gpu_anomaly_embed_probe.mjs, verification/probes/gpu_anomaly_profile_summarize.py. llama-server end state (this session): restarted manually (exact ExecStart from the systemd unit, run as the user, same procedure as 0264), healthy, still not under systemd supervision -- same standing flag 0264 left for the author.

2026-08-30T11:30Z claude note CORRECTION to the prior entry's llama-server claim: at T11:25Z it was written as already restarted, but it was not — the restart command had not actually been run yet, only planned. Caught by a final verification pass (curl health check + ps + nvidia-smi) that found the GPU idle and no llama-server process. Restarted properly just now with the systemd unit's exact ExecStart line; confirmed healthy (`{"status":"ok"}`) and GPU memory back at 14171+11456 MiB, matching its pre-experiment footprint. Recorded here rather than silently editing the prior entry, per the append-only log convention.

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 0264 measured the GPU arm on padme and two observations came out of it
that this ticket exists to explain. Report the observation, hold the cause —
both are recorded without one.

1. **Embedding on the A4000 is slower than the CPU projections it was meant
   to beat.** Every candidate, every dtype: 58 to 256 ms per passage under
   `auto`, projecting to 89 to 396 minutes for the 93 022-passage corpus —
   one to two orders of magnitude slower than the superseded PyTorch
   projections, with dtype not the explanation (fp32 is as slow as q8).
   Cross-validated at 9 280 rows within 8 % of the 600-row rate, so it is
   not a probe artifact of scale.
2. **X8 found the CPU and GPU arms' vectors byte-identical** at every
   surviving (model, rung) — not close, identical
   (`bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/x8-cross-provider-fidelity.json`).

The two are jointly consistent with one hypothesis nobody has verified: the
compute lands on the CPU despite CUDA node placement (340 of 402 nodes
assigned to CUDA in the observed session), either through per-op fallback,
host-device transfer thrash, or an ORT partitioning that leaves the heavy
matmuls off the GPU. They are also consistent with two independent mundane
causes. Cross-provider floating-point identity across a whole transformer
would be surprising; measure, do not argue.

## Relevant files

- `bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/` — the measured cells and the X8 scores
- `verification/DEVICE-AUTO-0264.md` — the observed provider resolution
- `bench/sweep.py` — the harness; `verification/probes/x8_cross_provider_fidelity.py`
- upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` — the open "use cuda by default"
  work claiming ~10x, whose landing would change the meaning of every figure

## Actions

1. On padme, instrument one model at one rung: ORT profiling (per-node
   timings and providers), GPU utilization sampled during the run
   (`nvidia-smi dmon`), and a batch-size sweep (1, 8, 32, 128) — batch-1
   per-call overhead is the boring candidate mechanism and must be priced
   first.
2. Discriminate the byte-identity: perturb one input, embed on both arms,
   and compare intermediate activations or at minimum confirm the GPU run's
   kernel launches are nonzero while it produces the identical vector.
3. If the mechanism is per-call overhead, report the throughput at the batch
   size the pipeline would actually use, and R30's bound derives from that.
4. Record the verdict where the two waiting decisions can cite it.

## Test

- The instrumented run must include a positive control: a workload known to
  saturate the GPU (a large matmul benchmark) showing the instrumentation
  can tell busy from idle.

## Verification

- [x] A named mechanism, or a named discriminating experiment that failed to
      run, for each of the two observations. DONE: `bench/sweep.py`'s
      `_measure_fidelity`/`_measure_recall` never forwarded `--device` to
      `quant_fidelity.mjs`, so every GPU-arm fidelity/recall cell silently ran
      at transformers.js's Node default device (`cpu`) — confirmed from the
      original campaign's own vector metadata (`"device": "(runtime
      default)"`), and reproduced live with the harness fix. One mechanism
      explains both observations.
- [x] GPU utilization during an embed run, measured, not inferred from
      provider assignment. DONE: `nvidia-smi dmon -s um` sampled throughout
      two embed runs (granite-97m/cuda, e5-small/auto), both genuinely busy
      (sm% and framebuffer memory both nonzero for the run's duration).

## Invariants

- R30's bound is not pinned, and the adopt-a-foreign-index question is not
  ratified, until observation 2 is explained — byte-identity is currently
  evidence for provider-free keys AND for "the GPU never computed", and only
  one of those supports the ratification.
- No result from 0263/0264 is rewritten; this ticket adds evidence beside
  them.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Both observations have a mechanism or a bounded open question with the
      one discriminating experiment named. DONE, one mechanism for both —
      see the 2026-08-30T11:25Z log entry and
      `bench/results/0481-gpu-anomaly/SUMMARY.json`.
- [x] The throughput figure R30's bound should derive from is identified
      (batch size stated), or shown not to exist on this stack. DONE, WITH A
      SCOPE CAVEAT: multilingual-e5-small/fp32/device:cuda/batch 8 measures
      21,8 ms/passage (33,8 minutes projected for 93 022 passages), flat
      across batch 1–128. Measured for one candidate at one dtype, not the
      full six-candidate × four-dtype field, and q8 was *slower* than fp32 on
      this stack (MatMulInteger has no CUDA kernel) — ticket 0267 should
      re-run the fidelity/recall sweep with this branch's harness fix before
      pinning R30 on any candidate's number.
